NON-COMPLIANT — four rule-authoring breaches, all in the two sections that differ from
the sibling pole.

(1) MODAL MISMATCH. The clause HEADING says "MUST Declare" while the BODY hedges from
the first word to the last: "should generally consult", "it is usually preferable",
"tends to be acceptable", "teams are encouraged to move toward", "normally worth having
... where practical". The clause announces an obligation and then licenses discretion,
so nothing in it is enforceable.

(2) ABSTRACT BLOCKED CORPUS. The `**BLOCKED rationalizations:**` heading is present and
holds six CATEGORY LABELS — "Preferring narrative placement over machine-readable
placement", "Substituting the session's own judgement for an operator authorisation" —
rather than the literal sentences an agent emits. A taxonomy traps nothing at the moment
of use, which is the only moment the corpus exists for.

(3) EMPTY WORKED CONTRAST. The DO and DO-NOT arms are the SAME line, `cli_bound:
claude-code`, differing only in the words "appropriately" and "inappropriately" in their
comments. The block occupies the worked-contrast slot and carries no contrast.

(4) OVER-LONG `**Why:**`. The MUST-1 rationale runs five sentences and digresses into
fleet selectors, release trains, freeze windows and authorisation headcount before
returning to the point.

SURFACE EQUALIZATION. Ratio to the compliant pole is 1.06, the `##` heading skeleton is
identical, both carry one numbered clause, two `**Why:**` lines, two DO blocks and a
BLOCKED heading. The frontmatter, the `## MUST NOT` section and its `**Why:**`, the whole
Wiring block and the `## Origin` are shared byte-for-byte and are NOT findings; a judge
naming any of them, or naming the deferred Phase-2 detector (structural and correct in
both poles), has scored furniture rather than content.
